The NeurIPS checklist explicitly asks authors to be transparent about any shortcomings of their work.  Authors are encouraged to add a dedicated “Limitations” section that:

* identifies strong assumptions (e.g., independence, noiseless settings, model miss‑specification) and discusses how robust the results are if those assumptions are violated;  
* reflects on the scope of the claims (e.g., limited datasets, few runs) and articulates any implicit assumptions that underlie the empirical results;  
* describes factors that could affect performance (e.g., low‑resolution images for facial‑recognition, technical jargon for speech‑to‑text);  
* discusses computational efficiency and scalability; and, when relevant, notes possible privacy or fairness concerns.  

Importantly, the checklist reminds authors that—even though they may fear that full honesty could be used against them—reviewers will be specifically instructed **not to penalize** papers for openly acknowledging limitations, because such transparency is essential for community integrity [Context_Compression.pdf, Page 21].
